                <pb facs="1302" n="1178" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg1178"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">467.11 W 89/32: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The High Commissioner at Constantinople</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Bristol</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-10-18T11:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 18,
                                1923—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 4:15
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>271. Your telegram 181 of September 29. I am informed by Adnan Bey that he has
                    been given full powers to negotiate with respect to American claims. He accepts
                    my suggestion to him of September 3 that the experts engage in informal
                    exploratory conversations before the formal negotiations begin. The Turks have
                    named as their experts Munir Bey who was at the Lausanne Conference and Ibrahim
                    Bey, financial inspector of the vilayet of Constantinople and former director
                    general of customs. Our experts will be Shaw and Turlington. The experts will
                    hold their first meeting on October 22 at the Sublime Porte. The Department may
                    wish to give me full powers to negotiate with respect to claims and also to send
                    me separate full powers to use in negotiations regarding naturalization if
                    occasion arises.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bristol</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
